course: dictionary
lesson: 5797
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer wants. «Mum was mad at me» means angry. «You're mad to swim in winter» means silly or foolish. Read the words around the word to decide.
explain: Ask yourself what fits. In «I'm mad about dinosaurs», mad means liking something very much, not being angry or ill. In «mad skills», it means very great or very good. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it.
explain: Some uses of mad are old-fashioned or offensive. Never use mad to mean mentally ill when talking about real people. Choose a clearer word instead, such as unwell or upset.
fact: Mad can mean angry, especially in the USA.
fact: Mad can mean very silly or crazy, in the UK and elsewhere.
fact: «Mad about» or «mad on» means liking something very much.
fact: Phrases like «go mad» and «drive someone mad» show the word in everyday talk.
fact: Mad as slang can mean very great, as in «mad skills».
exercise: «Mum was mad at me for being late.» What does mad mean here? | Silly | Happy | *Angry | Very great
exercise: «You're mad to swim in winter!» What does mad mean here? | Very great | Liking swimming very much | Angry with you | *Very silly or foolish
exercise: Ellie says, «I'm mad about dinosaurs.» What does mad mean? | Angry | Silly | *Liking something very much | Very great
test: «She is mad about chess and plays every day.» What does mad mean here? | Very cold | Very angry | Foolish | *Liking something very much
test: «Those skateboard tricks are mad skills!» What does mad mean here? | Angry | *Very great | Sad | Silly
exam: Ruby says, «Stop it, you're driving me mad!» What does she mean? | She is very happy | She is ill and needs a doctor | *She is very annoyed with you | She is going swimming
